{"arm": "X2-P", "compounds": 0, "compounds_kept": 0, "diagnoses": [{"cell": 2, "cost_per_unit": "Under the profiler the run takes about 3.77 s in total, which is roughly 57 us per token. Grouping makes about 11 match/imt-style calls and 11 flatten resumptions per token. On average each token also has 7 to 8 type-membership checks.", "deal_round": 1, "doubling": "Doubling the number of statements roughly doubles wall time, because the cost is linear in tokens. Within one statement, rebuilding str() for each new group costs subtree size times nesting depth, so deeper nesting grows slightly superlinearly.", "gap": "Several _group_matching passes (brackets, IF, FOR, BEGIN) scan the full tree even though the statement contains no opener. Group construction re-flattens subtrees whose strings are already stored in child .value. Each Token re-tests ttype membership by tuple slicing although only about 30 distinct t", "least_work": "Lex each statement once. Classify each token's type flags once per distinct ttype. Make one cheap visit per token for each pass that can actually fire. Derive each new group's text from values its children already hold.", "library": "sqlparse", "run": 3, "unit_count": "About 66k token yields from get_tokens, across three lexes of the script: split, format and parse. Token.match is called 751k times, imt 524k, tokens.__contains__ 499k and flatten 743k generator calls.", "unit_of_work": "One lexed token. Each token is lexed, wrapped in a sql.Token by the splitter, then visited by about 25 grouping passes. Each pass walks the whole tree recursively and calls match, imt or __contains__ on every token. Every group the passes create also runs str(self), which flattens its whole subtree."}], "experiment": "ideas-x2-round", "kept": 3, "lane_views": [{"lane": "obvious", "served_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "view_sha": "3abcfdec"}, {"lane": "name_the_problem", "served_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "view_sha": "3abcfdec"}, {"lane": "leap", "served_model": "c